## fast-tests (hunkim)

Parts of this skill are adapted from the `fast-tests` skill of
"AI is fast. Your tests are slow." by Sung Kim.

| Item | Value |
|---|---|
| Source | <https://github.com/hunkim/ai-is-fast-your-tests-are-slow> |
| Version | commit `9b076d08ffb5e6dd1e652b67984d533a6251810b` |
| License | MIT (reproduced below) |
| Adapted here | `references/validation-cost.md` (slow test suite procedure), `references/test-waits.md`, `references/test-parallelism.md`, `references/test-selection.md`, `references/flaky-tests.md`, `references/test-evidence.md`, the flaky-test and compact-output paragraphs of `references/local-feedback.md`, `scripts/test_profile.ts`, `scripts/test_smells.ts` |

## Local changes

- The fast-tests material is reorganized under jstack's owning skills and
  rewritten. No file from the fast-tests source is copied verbatim.
- Local runs follow jstack's affected-check scope; the required gate runs the
  complete suite. The upstream rule to run the complete suite before and after
  every change is not adopted.
- Figures are attributed to their primary sources and marked as not reproduced
  by jstack.
- `scripts/test_profile.js` and `scripts/test_smells.js` are TypeScript ports of
  `profile_tests.py` and `scan_test_smells.py`. CPU time comes from bash's
  `time` keyword instead of `os.wait4`, and is reported as unmeasured without
  bash. The profiler accepts `--waiting-ratio` and `--waiting-min-seconds`;
  the scanner skips lines marked `test-smells: allow` with a reason.

